+# Fine-tuned vs. Default GR00T N1.7, and Why SONIC Needs Fine-tuning
+
+This note summarizes, at the level of model structure / inputs / outputs, how a
+fine-tuned GR00T N1.7 differs from the default (pretrained base) model, and why
+driving the Unitree G1 through the **GEAR-SONIC** whole-body controller (WBC)
+requires fine-tuning rather than the default checkpoint.
+
+All statements are grounded in the code:
+
+- Model: [`gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py`](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py)
+- Config: [`gr00t/configs/model/gr00t_n1d7.py`](../gr00t/configs/model/gr00t_n1d7.py)
+- Per-embodiment head: [`gr00t/model/modules/embodiment_conditioned_mlp.py`](../gr00t/model/modules/embodiment_conditioned_mlp.py)
+- Embodiment modalities: [`gr00t/configs/data/embodiment_configs.py`](../gr00t/configs/data/embodiment_configs.py)
+- Embodiment IDs: [`gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/processing_gr00t_n1d7.py`](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/processing_gr00t_n1d7.py) (`EMBODIMENT_TAG_TO_PROJECTOR_INDEX`)
+- Fine-tune config: [`gr00t/configs/finetune_config.py`](../gr00t/configs/finetune_config.py)
+
+---
+
+## 1. The architecture is identical — fine-tuning does not change structure
+
+GR00T N1.7 is a single end-to-end VLA. There is **no separate "token → whole-body
+decoder"** stage inside this repo:
+
+```
+video + language ─►  VLM backbone (Qwen3-VL) ─┐
+                                                ├─► action head ─► action vector
+state + noised_action + embodiment_id ────────┘   (flow-matching DiT)
+```
+
+The action head is a flow-matching diffusion transformer (`AlternateVLDiT`) plus
+three thin **per-embodiment** MLPs: `state_encoder`, `action_encoder`,
+`action_decoder`.
+
+Fine-tuning loads the **same** `Gr00tN1d7Config` from the base checkpoint and only
+flips a few `tune_*` switches — **no layer is added or removed**. So the structural
+difference between "default" and "fine-tuned" is: **none**. What actually changes is
+described below.
+
+---
+
+## 2. What fine-tuning actually changes
+
+### (A) Trainable parameter set
+
+Controlled by `set_trainable_parameters` ([gr00t_n1d7.py:107](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py#L107)).
+Fine-tune defaults ([finetune_config.py](../gr00t/configs/finetune_config.py)):
+
+| Module | Default fine-tune | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `tune_llm` / `tune_visual` | **False** | VLM backbone frozen |
+| `tune_projector` | **True** | state/action encoders + action decoder train |
+| `tune_diffusion_model` | **True** | shared DiT trunk trains |
+| `tune_vlln` | True | VL layernorm + VL self-attention |
+
+I.e. freeze the vision-language "brain", train the action side.
+
+### (B) The per-embodiment head slice
+
+The only per-embodiment weights are `state_encoder`, `action_encoder`,
+`action_decoder`. Each is a `CategorySpecificLinear` indexed by `embodiment_id`
+(`W[cat_id]`), with a leading `num_categories = max_num_embodiments = 32` dimension.
+The **DiT trunk and the backbone are shared across all embodiments** — only these
+thin MLP slices are private.
+
+Fine-tuning on a robot trains **that robot's slice** (plus the shared DiT), writing
+its state/action semantics into one `W[cat_id]`.
+
+### (C) Input/output semantics (defined by the modality config, not by layer shapes)
+
+Every embodiment pads to `max_state_dim = max_action_dim = 132`. Which of those 132
+dims are real, and what they mean, comes entirely from the embodiment's
+`ModalityConfig` + dataset `modality.json`. Padding dims are zeroed and excluded from
+the loss via `action_mask` ([gr00t_n1d7.py:262](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py#L262)).
+
+### Default vs. fine-tuned at a glance
+
+| | Default (pretrained base) | Fine-tuned |
+|---|---|---|
+| Architecture / backbone / DiT | same | same |
+| Trainable params | — | projector + DiT + vlln (LLM/visual frozen) |
+| Embodiment coverage | broad multi-embodiment mix baked in | one target `embodiment_id` slice specialized |
+| Action semantics | whatever the pretrain modalities were | the target dataset's action modality |
+| Data | large mixed corpus | your demonstrations |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Concrete tensor contract
+
+Input to the action head ([gr00t_n1d7.py:168-180](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py#L168-L180)):
+
+- `state`: `[B, state_history_length, 132]` (real dims left-aligned, rest zero-padded)
+- `action` (training target): `[B, action_horizon, 132]`
+- `action_mask`: 1 on real dims only → loss only on real action dims
+- `embodiment_id`: `[B]`
+
+Output:
+
+- Training: flow-matching velocity MSE loss (`velocity = action − noise`)
+- Inference `get_action`: 4-step Euler denoising → `action_pred [B, action_horizon, action_dim]`
+  ([gr00t_n1d7.py:383-429](../gr00t/model/gr00t_n1d7/gr00t_n1d7.py#L383-L429)). This
+  vector **is** the robot action for that embodiment (for SONIC: the latent + finger joints).
+
+Note: state is heavily regularized out during training (`state_dropout_prob` default
+0.8, plus an `exclude_state` ablation, [config:117-118](../gr00t/configs/model/gr00t_n1d7.py#L117-L118)).
+So lower-body / gravity information is learned primarily as an **action supervision
+target**, not as a hard encoder input requirement.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Example: `libero_sim` vs. `unitree_g1_sonic` head slices
+
+| | `libero_sim` (id = 2) | `unitree_g1_sonic` (id = 11) |
+|---|---|---|
+| state keys | x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw, gripper | left_leg, right_leg, waist, left_arm, right_arm, left_hand, right_hand, **projected_gravity** |
+| state real dims | **8** → padded to 132 | full-body joints + gravity (≫ 8) → padded to 132 |
+| action keys | x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw, gripper | **motion_token**, left_hand_joints, right_hand_joints |
+| action real dims | **7** → padded to 132 | SONIC whole-body latent + finger joints → padded to 132 |
+| action semantics | single-arm EEF delta + gripper | **whole-body WBC latent (SONIC)** + fingers |
+| horizon (`delta_indices`) | `range(16)` | `range(40)` |
+| cameras | image + wrist_image (2) | ego_view + left_wrist + right_wrist (3) |
+
+The two slices have **identical tensor shape** but **independent weights** and
+**different physical meaning**. They share the DiT trunk and backbone; specialization
+lives in the thin MLP slice + the data.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Why interfacing with SONIC requires fine-tuning
+
+GEAR-SONIC is a whole-body controller whose command interface is a **latent action**
+(`motion_token`) — a compact code that encodes coordinated legs + waist + arms + root
+locomotion, which SONIC then expands into low-level joint commands. The
+`unitree_g1_sonic` embodiment is defined exactly this way: *"VLA action space is SONIC
+latents"* ([embodiment_tags.py:107](../gr00t/data/embodiment_tags.py#L107)).
+
+The default base checkpoint cannot drive SONIC, for three concrete reasons:
+
+1. **The output channel does not exist.** Default manipulation embodiments emit
+   arm-EEF / gripper vectors (e.g. libero's 7 dims). None of their action modalities
+   contain a `motion_token` output. It is not that lower-body signal is "zeroed out" —
+   there is simply **no latent-action output dimension** for SONIC to consume. Hard-
+   aligning dimensions would only produce zero/garbage in the SONIC latent slots, i.e.
+   a degenerate, under-specified command.
+
+2. **No trained head slice for that embodiment.** `motion_token` decoding lives in the
+   `unitree_g1_sonic` slice (`W[11]`) of the action encoder/decoder. In the base model
+   that slice is untrained / out-of-distribution, so even with the right modality wired
+   up it would not produce a valid latent.
+
+3. **Task-level whole-body intent must be learned.** A latent that means "step / turn /
+   shift the center of mass to support the arm motion" only exists if the model was
+   trained on whole-body demonstrations. That coordination has to be written into the
+   64-dim-style latent output; the shared DiT + the `unitree_g1_sonic` slice learn it
+   during fine-tuning.
+
+**Therefore:** to talk to SONIC you fine-tune GR00T N1.7 on the `unitree_g1_sonic`
+embodiment with whole-body demonstration data whose action labels are the SONIC
+latents. This (a) selects the action modality that includes `motion_token`,
+(b) trains that embodiment's head slice + the shared DiT to map VL features into the
+SONIC latent space, and (c) leaves the architecture, backbone, and inference pipeline
+unchanged. The VLA then emits the whole-body latent directly, and SONIC executes it —
+closing the gap that the upper-body-only default model cannot.
+
+**In one line:** the default model has no SONIC-latent output channel and no trained
+slice for it; fine-tuning gives the VLA that output channel and teaches whole-body /
+locomotion coordination inside the latent, without changing the model structure.
